      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval",
      "problem": "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places.",
